      "id": 1,
      "text": "Interior is located along the White River approximately one mile from Badlands National Park.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interior%2C_South_Dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Norwegian immigrants George and Louis Johnson established a freighting way-station here in 1883.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interior%2C_South_Dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "George Johnson renamed the town Interior in 1893 because he felt the previous name, Black, did not fit the White River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interior%2C_South_Dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Interior moved its entire location about two miles northwest between 1907 and 1908 to be adjacent to the Milwaukee Railroad tracks.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/interior_south_dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town installed an efficient water system transporting water directly from the White River in 1910.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interior%2C_South_Dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "In 1919, Interior hosted roundups that attracted tourists traveling by train from as far away as Minneapolis.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
